import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.Dkb1AhHj.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Implementation","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/implementation.md","filePath":"explanation/implementation.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"explanation/implementation.md"};function i(r,e,l,d,h,c){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="Implementation" tabindex="-1">Implementation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Implementation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Implementation {#Implementation}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR33/explanation/algorithms#Algorithms">algorithms</a> explain what is computed. This page explains how the code is organised and why, for someone who wants to change it.</p><h2 id="Specialisation-by-type-for-the-codelets,-data-for-the-plan" tabindex="-1">Specialisation by type for the codelets, data for the plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#Specialisation-by-type-for-the-codelets,-data-for-the-plan" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Specialisation by type for the codelets, data for the plan {#Specialisation-by-type-for-the-codelets,-data-for-the-plan}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A codelet is specialised by type: the stage kernels take the radix, the sign and the vector type as type parameters, and the straight-line code of each combination is compiled once. The combinations form a fixed set (radix × sign × vector type × element type), so all of them are compiled with the package.</p><p>The stage sequence of a length is data. A Stockham chain (<code>RChain</code>, <code>src/rchain.jl</code>) holds its radix sequence and one record per stage (codelet index, stride, twiddle table), and a loop calls the codelet of each stage. Nothing is compiled when a plan of a new length is made.</p><h2 id="Codelets-on-SIMD-vectors" tabindex="-1">Codelets on SIMD vectors <a class="header-anchor" href="#Codelets-on-SIMD-vectors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Codelets on SIMD vectors {#Codelets-on-SIMD-vectors}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The arithmetic of a codelet is written on complex SIMD vectors (<code>src/complexvec.jl</code>). A vector holds several complex values, and the same instruction stream runs on all of them. For a <code>Complex{Float32}</code> array on a 128-bit machine that is two values per register, and eight with AVX-512. Lanes of the multidimensional transforms are the vector elements, so the same codelet serves a single line and a batch of lines.</p><p>The scalar versions in <code>src/ka.jl</code> run the same generated codelets on plain <code>Complex</code> values. That is how the KernelAbstractions kernels use the codelet generator without a second implementation.</p><h2 id="Kernel-cache" tabindex="-1">Kernel cache <a class="header-anchor" href="#Kernel-cache" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kernel cache {#Kernel-cache}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Kernels are cached by kind, element type, length, sign and radix sequence. The cache has 128 entries and drops the least recently used one when it is full. A Bluestein kernel is two entries, its chain and its tables. <code>clear_cache!</code> empties it. Within one plan, dimensions with the same length and stride class share a kernel object.</p><h2 id="Cache-sizes-and-blocks" tabindex="-1">Cache sizes and blocks <a class="header-anchor" href="#Cache-sizes-and-blocks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cache sizes and blocks {#Cache-sizes-and-blocks}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The work of a batched transform is split into blocks of lines so that the work buffers of one block stay in the cache of one core. The cache sizes are read when the package loads: <code>sysctl</code> on macOS for the performance cores, <code>sysfs</code> on Linux for CPU 0. Where the query fails, defaults for the two development machines stand in. <code>FFTL.cpu_caches()</code> reports the values, and <code>FFTL.set_cpu_caches!</code> overrides them for a session.</p><p>The thresholds that decide between one thread and several, between the lane and the plane forms, and between the four-step and the plain algorithm live in one place, <code>FFTL.TUNING</code> (<code>src/tuning.jl</code>). Changing one changes the plans made afterwards.</p><h2 id="Thread-tasks-and-buffers" tabindex="-1">Thread tasks and buffers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Thread-tasks-and-buffers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Thread tasks and buffers {#Thread-tasks-and-buffers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Threads come from Julia&#39;s task scheduler. A threaded pass hands chunks of its work to a fixed number of tasks, and each task owns its own buffers, allocated when the plan is made. Two buffers per task are the minimum: the Stockham stages ping-pong between them.</p><p>That buffer ownership is why a plan must not run on two tasks at once. The plan is a container for the buffers, not a stateless function.</p><h2 id="The-stage-loop" tabindex="-1">The stage loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-stage-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The stage loop {#The-stage-loop}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>RChain</code> calls codelets from the fixed set in a plain loop, selected by an integer index. A short line is a few nanoseconds of arithmetic, so the loop is written to cost little next to it:</p><ul><li><p>A line that is one stage is one codelet call, without the loop.</p></li><li><p>The stage records lie in one flat vector and carry the address of their twiddle table, and the loop reads them through a raw pointer. A reference loaded from the chain inside the loop would need a GC frame, which costs more than a short stage.</p></li><li><p>The chain is a mutable struct, so it is passed as one pointer; an immutable chain is copied to the stack of each caller.</p></li><li><p>The switch over the codelet index is inlined into the loop: a stage is one jump and one call.</p></li><li><p>The rows of a plane run inside one call of the loop function.</p></li></ul><p>A stage of a prime radix from 17 on calls one kernel for all such radices (<code>prime_stage!</code> in <code>src/primestage.jl</code>), with the radix and its table as arguments; it is compiled once per vector type. Its calls sit behind a function of their own, outside the switch, because they take more arguments than fit in registers and would cost every case of the switch a larger stack frame.</p><p>The addresses in the stage records are valid for the chain object they were taken for. A chain remembers its own address at that moment; a copy of a plan, or a plan stored in a package image and loaded into another process, is at another address and links its records again before it uses them.</p><p><code>RChain</code> is also what the C library is built from. <code>juliac --trim</code> removes everything it cannot resolve at build time and compiles nothing at run time; the fixed codelet set is all a chain of any length needs. The <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR33/howto/libfftl#C-library-(libfftl)">libfftl how-to</a> describes what the library is built from, and <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR33/explanation/design-decisions#Design-decisions">Design decisions</a> gives the measurements behind the single chain form. The stage kernels of the fixed codelet set are plain methods defined with <code>@eval</code>, not <code>@generated</code> ones, to keep the package image small; see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR33/explanation/image-size#Package-size-and-load-time">Package size and load time</a>.</p><h2 id="Lengths-and-the-factorisation-rule" tabindex="-1">Lengths and the factorisation rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#Lengths-and-the-factorisation-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lengths and the factorisation rule {#Lengths-and-the-factorisation-rule}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>src/factor.jl</code> holds the rule that turns a length into radices. Its header comment records the measurements the rule was derived from, and the constants in it are not tuned at run time. A change to the rule needs a measurement on the machines it is meant for: the rule that is best on one architecture can be slower on another.</p><h2 id="Strided-layouts" tabindex="-1">Strided layouts <a class="header-anchor" href="#Strided-layouts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Strided layouts {#Strided-layouts}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Complex plans fix the shape, but read the input and output strides independently on every call. Contiguous views use the dense executors, including plane fusion. Other strided layouts merge leading dimensions into lanes only while both operands are contiguous there; the remaining dimensions supply independent batch offsets. This avoids full-array copies without adding plan storage. Arrays outside <code>StridedArray</code> retain the copying fallback.</p><p><code>LinesMT</code> owns a child kernel and scratch buffers per task. The strided executor uses that task&#39;s buffers and never passes more lanes than the child was built to hold. For a single strided <code>Composite</code> line, it gathers the input into the plan&#39;s separate task scratch when that scratch is large enough. A packed input lets the first stage process blocks instead of one small transform per leftover element. The output is written directly with its own stride; insufficient scratch keeps the direct strided path.</p><p>On an Apple M5 Max, macOS arm64, Julia 1.13.3 and two threads, <code>Float32</code> forward transforms of <code>65×2018</code> views along dimension 2, with input step 2 and output step 3, took a median 469.80 µs against 622.13 µs for the copying fallback at <code>289e9c6</code>. Forward, backward and normalized inverse times were 24–25% lower; the maximum paired A/A differences were 1.96–3.77% across those cases. These measurements used eight interleaved rounds in separate warmed processes. Threaded calls allocated 592 bytes; gathering adds no plan storage. This is evidence for these layouts on this processor, not a claim for every strided transform.</p><h3 id="Real-plans" tabindex="-1">Real plans <a class="header-anchor" href="#Real-plans" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Real plans {#Real-plans}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>High-level real plans retain their dense offsets and refill separate input and output offset vectors for strided execution. Keeping the two sets separate allows a plan to alternate between dense arrays and views without corrupting its dense layout. The extra payload is <code>2 * number_of_lines * sizeof(Int)</code>, or 64 bytes for four lines on a 64-bit machine, plus vector metadata. The inverse spectrum scratch is allocated in its final shape during planning, so preserving the input during complex passes needs no execution-time reshape or extra element storage.</p><p>On the same processor and Julia version, with one thread, a <code>Float64</code> forward <code>10240×4</code> column block took 43.39 µs and allocated zero bytes. The explicit dense-copy workaround at <code>289e9c6</code> took 59.85 µs and allocated 671,904 bytes; native real-view <code>mul!</code> is unavailable at that baseline. Eight interleaved rounds gave a 27.5% time reduction, against a maximum paired A/A difference of 2.41%. The <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR33/howto/real#Real-data">real-data guide</a> shows the layout contract and an executable example.</p><h2 id="Element-types" tabindex="-1">Element types <a class="header-anchor" href="#Element-types" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Element types {#Element-types}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The fast path is written for <code>Float32</code> and <code>Float64</code>. Other floating-point types use <code>GPlan</code> (<code>src/generic.jl</code>): a recursive mixed-radix transform on plain arrays, with Bluestein&#39;s algorithm for large prime factors, twiddles computed in the type itself, no SIMD and no threads. It is much slower, and its purpose is accuracy and generality.</p><h2 id="The-GPU-path" tabindex="-1">The GPU path <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-GPU-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The GPU path {#The-GPU-path}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>ext/FFTLKernelAbstractionsExt.jl</code> holds the KernelAbstractions extension. Its kernels compute one pass of a transform in workgroup memory. The pass sizes follow the workgroup memory the device offers; the rules were measured on an Apple M1 Max, and the CUDA path on an RTX A6000.</p>',35)])])}const u=a(n,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
